ntdll, server: Haiku: the Unix thread ID, so the server can signal a thread

On Haiku get_unix_tid() returned -1 (no gettid(), no
pthread_getthreadid_np()), so the server knew no thread of any Wine
process and send_thread_signal() always failed: it could never interrupt
a thread that was not waiting for it. A system APC (an asynchronous I/O
completion, a cross-process virtual memory call) for a busy thread could
not be queued, SuspendThread() of a running thread did not stop it,
GetThreadContext() of it never returned, and TerminateThread() of it left
it running. A cancelled overlapped read never filled in its OVERLAPPED
(GetOverlappedResult() kept reporting it pending), and one such failed
completion made cancel_process_async() use a freed async.

ntdll now reports find_thread(NULL), Haiku's system-wide thread ID, as the
thread's Unix tid, and the server signals that one thread with Haiku's
send_signal() (kill() would deliver the signal to any thread of the
team). Thread IDs come from a counter that is not reused until it wraps
at 2^31, so the server needs no check that the thread is still in the
process, as tgkill() makes. A thread that is gone yields ESRCH, so the
server forgets its tid as on Linux; the ENOSYS fallback to kill() is never
taken. GetThreadTimes() and the per-thread times of
NtQuerySystemInformation() work too now (get_thread_times() had a Haiku
path that the -1 tid never reached).

--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/server.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/server.c
@@ -72,6 +72,13 @@
 #endif
 #ifdef __HAIKU__
 #define SIGIO SIGPOLL
+#define cpu_info haiku_cpu_info
+#define exit_thread haiku_exit_thread
+#define spawn_thread haiku_spawn_thread
+#include <OS.h>
+#undef cpu_info
+#undef exit_thread
+#undef spawn_thread
 #endif
 
 #include "ntstatus.h"
@@ -1549,6 +1556,8 @@
     ret = lwpid;
 #elif defined(__DragonFly__)
     ret = lwp_gettid();
+#elif defined(__HAIKU__)
+    ret = find_thread( NULL );
 #endif
     return ret;
 }
--- a/server/ptrace.c
+++ b/server/ptrace.c
@@ -47,6 +47,10 @@
 # include <sys/uio.h>
 #endif
 #include <unistd.h>
+#ifdef __HAIKU__
+/* from OS.h, whose thread functions clash with the server's (kill_thread(), suspend_thread()...) */
+extern int send_signal( int thread, unsigned int sig );
+#endif
 
 #include "ntstatus.h"
 #include "winternl.h"
@@ -226,6 +230,14 @@
     return ret;
 #elif (defined(__FreeBSD__) || defined (__FreeBSD_kernel__)) && defined(HAVE_THR_KILL2)
     return thr_kill2( tgid, pid, sig );
+#elif defined(__HAIKU__)
+    /* send_signal() signals that thread only (kill() would pick any thread of the team). Thread IDs are
+     * system-wide and not reused until the kernel's 31-bit counter wraps, so tgid needs no check */
+    int status = send_signal( pid, sig );
+
+    if (status == B_OK) return 0;
+    errno = status == B_BAD_THREAD_ID ? ESRCH : status;
+    return -1;
 #else
     errno = ENOSYS;
     return -1;
